# Plover App Constitution

## Core Principles

### I. Feature-Based Architecture (MANDATORY)

**Every feature MUST be organized in a feature-based folder structure with the following layers:**

```
src/features/[feature-name]/
├── repository/          # Data access layer - abstracts data sources (API, local storage, cache)
│   ├── [feature].repository.ts
│   └── [feature].repository.interface.ts
├── service/            # Business logic layer - contains use cases and domain logic
│   ├── [feature].service.ts
│   └── types.ts
├── hooks/              # React hooks for feature-specific state management and side effects
│   ├── use[Feature].ts
│   └── use[Feature]Data.ts
├── components/         # Feature-specific UI components
│   ├── [Feature]Component.tsx
│   └── [Feature]List.tsx
└── pages/              # Feature pages/screens (Expo Router pages)
    └── [feature].tsx
```

**Rules:**
- Features must be self-contained with clear boundaries
- Cross-feature dependencies must go through well-defined interfaces
- Shared components go in `src/components/`, not within features
- Each feature directory must include all layers (repository, service, hooks, components, pages)
- Features communicate through services, never directly access other features' repositories

### II. Code Quality Standards

**Type Safety:**
- TypeScript strict mode is mandatory
- All functions, components, and data structures must have explicit types
- Avoid `any` - use `unknown` with type guards when necessary
- Define interfaces for all data models, API responses, and component props
- Use discriminated unions for state management

**Code Organization:**
- Single Responsibility Principle: Each function/component does one thing well
- DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself): Extract shared logic into utilities or hooks
- KISS (Keep It Simple, Stupid): Prefer simple solutions over complex abstractions
- Maximum function length: 50 lines (extract helpers if exceeded)
- Maximum component length: 200 lines (split into smaller components)
- Maximum file length: 300 lines (split into modules)

**Naming Conventions:**
- Components: PascalCase (e.g., `UserProfile`, `ProductCard`)
- Functions/Variables: camelCase (e.g., `fetchUserData`, `isLoading`)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `API_BASE_URL`, `MAX_RETRY_COUNT`)
- Types/Interfaces: PascalCase with descriptive names (e.g., `UserProfile`, `ApiResponse<T>`)
- Files: kebab-case for utilities, PascalCase for components (e.g., `user-utils.ts`, `UserProfile.tsx`)
- Feature directories: kebab-case (e.g., `user-profile`, `product-catalog`)

**Code Style:**
- Use functional components with hooks exclusively
- Prefer arrow functions for callbacks and event handlers
- Use destructuring for props and state
- Use optional chaining (`?.`) and nullish coalescing (`??`) appropriately
- Format code with Prettier (enforced via ESLint)
- Maximum nesting depth: 3 levels (extract functions if deeper)

**Error Handling:**
- All async operations must have error handling
- Use try-catch blocks for error boundaries
- Provide meaningful error messages to users
- Log errors appropriately (never log sensitive data)
- Use Result/Either patterns for operations that can fail

### III. Testing Standards (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

**Test Coverage Requirements:**
- Minimum 80% code coverage for services and repositories
- Minimum 70% code coverage for hooks
- Minimum 60% code coverage for components
- 100% coverage for critical business logic (authentication, payments, data validation)

**Test Types:**
1. **Unit Tests**: Test individual functions, services, and utilities in isolation
   - Location: `src/features/[feature]/__tests__/`
   - Naming: `[module].test.ts` or `[module].spec.ts`
   - Mock external dependencies (API calls, storage, etc.)

2. **Integration Tests**: Test feature workflows and service interactions
   - Location: `src/features/[feature]/__tests__/integration/`
   - Test complete user flows within a feature
   - Use test databases/mocks for data access

3. **Component Tests**: Test React components in isolation
   - Use React Native Testing Library
   - Test user interactions, rendering, and accessibility
   - Location: `src/features/[feature]/components/__tests__/`

4. **E2E Tests**: Test complete user journeys across features
   - Use Detox or similar E2E framework
   - Location: `e2e/`
   - Cover critical user paths (onboarding, core workflows)

**Testing Best Practices:**
- Write tests BEFORE implementation (TDD when possible)
- Tests must be independent and runnable in any order
- Use descriptive test names: `describe('FeatureName', () => { it('should do X when Y', () => {}) })`
- Arrange-Act-Assert pattern for test structure
- Mock external dependencies (APIs, native modules, timers)
- Test edge cases and error scenarios
- Keep tests maintainable - if a test is hard to write, simplify the code

**Test Quality Gates:**
- All tests must pass before merging to main
- No skipped tests without documented justification
- Flaky tests must be fixed immediately
- Test execution time: Unit tests < 5s, Integration tests < 30s per suite

### IV. User Experience Consistency

**Design System:**
- Use a centralized theme system (`src/constants/theme.ts`)
- All colors, typography, spacing, and shadows must come from the theme
- Support light and dark modes consistently
- Use semantic color names (e.g., `primary`, `error`, `success`) not literal colors (e.g., `blue500`)

**Component Consistency:**
- Reusable components must be in `src/components/` or `src/components/ui/`
- Feature-specific components in feature directories
- Components must be accessible (screen reader support, proper labels)
- Consistent loading states, error states, and empty states
- Standardized button styles, input styles, and form layouts

**Navigation & Flow:**
- Use Expo Router for all navigation
- Consistent navigation patterns across features
- Clear back navigation and breadcrumbs where appropriate
- Loading indicators during async operations
- Success/error feedback for user actions

**Responsive Design:**
- Support multiple screen sizes (phone, tablet)
- Test on iOS and Android
- Use responsive units and flexible layouts
- Handle keyboard appearance gracefully
- Safe area insets for notched devices

**Performance Perception:**
- Show loading states immediately (< 100ms)
- Skeleton screens for content loading
- Optimistic UI updates where appropriate
- Smooth animations (60fps target)
- No layout shifts during loading

**Accessibility:**
- All interactive elements must be accessible
- Proper semantic HTML/React Native components
- Screen reader labels for all images and icons
- Minimum touch target size: 44x44 points
- Sufficient color contrast (WCAG AA minimum)

### V. Performance Requirements

**Rendering Performance:**
- Maintain 60 FPS during scrolling and animations
- Use `React.memo()` for expensive components
- Implement `useMemo()` and `useCallback()` for expensive computations
- Virtualize long lists (FlatList with proper optimization props)
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders (use React DevTools Profiler)

**Bundle Size:**
- Monitor bundle size with each release
- Code split by route/feature where possible
- Lazy load heavy dependencies
- Tree-shake unused code
- Maximum initial bundle size: 2MB (gzipped)

**Network Performance:**
- Implement request caching (React Query, SWR, or similar)
- Debounce/throttle user input that triggers API calls
- Paginate large data sets
- Compress images and use appropriate formats (WebP when supported)
- Maximum API response time: 2s (p95)
- Implement offline support for critical features

**Memory Management:**
- Clean up subscriptions and timers in useEffect cleanup
- Unmount unused components
- Limit image cache size
- Profile memory usage regularly
- Maximum memory usage: 150MB on average devices

**Startup Performance:**
- App launch time: < 3s on average devices
- Time to interactive: < 2s
- Optimize splash screen and initial render
- Lazy load non-critical features

**Monitoring:**
- Track performance metrics in production
- Monitor crash rates (target: < 0.1%)
- Track API response times and error rates
- Monitor bundle size trends
- Set up performance budgets and alerts

## Architecture Patterns

### Repository Pattern
- **Purpose**: Abstract data access from business logic
- **Implementation**: 
  - Define interfaces in `repository/[feature].repository.interface.ts`
  - Implement concrete repositories (API, LocalStorage, Cache)
  - Services depend on repository interfaces, not implementations
- **Benefits**: Easy to swap data sources, testable, clear separation of concerns

### Service Layer Pattern
- **Purpose**: Encapsulate business logic and use cases
- **Implementation**:
  - Services contain domain logic and orchestration
  - Services use repositories for data access
  - Services are pure functions when possible (easier to test)
- **Benefits**: Reusable business logic, testable without UI, clear API

### Custom Hooks Pattern
- **Purpose**: Encapsulate stateful logic and side effects
- **Implementation**:
  - Hooks call services and manage component state
  - Hooks handle loading, error, and success states
  - Hooks expose clean APIs to components
- **Benefits**: Reusable state logic, separation of concerns, testable

## Quality Gates

**Before Code Review:**
- [ ] All tests pass locally
- [ ] Code coverage meets minimum requirements
- [ ] No linter errors or warnings
- [ ] TypeScript compiles without errors
- [ ] Manual testing on iOS and Android
- [ ] Performance benchmarks met (if applicable)

**Before Merge to Main:**
- [ ] Code review approved by at least one team member
- [ ] All CI/CD checks pass
- [ ] Feature works on both iOS and Android
- [ ] Accessibility requirements met
- [ ] Performance requirements met
- [ ] Documentation updated (if needed)

**Before Release:**
- [ ] E2E tests pass
- [ ] Performance regression tests pass
- [ ] Security review completed (if handling sensitive data)
- [ ] Changelog updated
- [ ] Version bumped appropriately

## Governance

**Constitution Compliance:**
- This constitution supersedes all other coding guidelines
- All code must comply with these principles
- Violations require explicit justification and approval
- Regular architecture reviews to ensure compliance

**Amendments:**
- Amendments require team discussion and consensus
- Document rationale for any changes
- Update this document with version history
- Communicate changes to all team members

**Enforcement:**
- Automated checks via CI/CD where possible
- Code review process validates compliance
- Architecture decision records (ADRs) for exceptions
- Regular team retrospectives on architecture

